WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
Welcome to the very first episode of the DP Pod, the due positive podcast.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:12.020
Today is July 4th, 2026. Two friends built a 14-show daily podcast network with an AI pipeline

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:17.720
and no studio or staff. We actually did that. We did. And now we're sitting here talking about it

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.280
on the first show where the two of us are the ones in front of the mic. Feels weird in the best way.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.040
The feeds are built to make everyone feel powerless. We got tired of that.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.940
So we started from the opposite bed. Individuals are not powerless. 10 minutes a day of real

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:37.720
movement can prove it. That's the whole club right there. The Narrow Network, Novak Plus Para.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:44.600
We kept adding shows until we hit 14 daily ones. All add free, all independent, all running on one

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:50.280
pipeline we built ourselves. This episode is the 14th. The first dialogue show and the first time

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:55.300
we step out from behind the scripts. It's for builders. People already trying to move something

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:00.820
forward and people who want to start. We review what actually matters and celebrate the people

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:06.640
building through it. The pledge gets you in at NarrowNetwork.com. Then there's the lever each week

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.140
and the dispatch where we read what listeners actually did. We want to turn de-groom scrolling into a

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:17.680
daily habit of acting instead. One real thing, then another. That brings us to think positive.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.640
Tony Robbins has pointed out for years that motivation follows action. Waiting to feel ready

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.700
keeps most people stuck. Move first and the energy shows up after. Does that hold when the days

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:35.280
already gone sideways? The network itself is the test case. Every working piece came after an

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:41.700
earlier imperfect attempt. The mental rep is pick one task you've been postponing. Set a two-minute

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:47.780
timer and begin anyway. Simple enough to actually do. Time for the positive papers. The story we're

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.940
telling is our own build. Two people asked whether they could run what used to need an entire company.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:58.800
The answer turned into 14 shows. Started with separate scripts, thousands of lines each glued

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:06.200
together by cron jobs. That duplicated everything, so we refactored into one runner, one config per show,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.680
and a shared engine. Adding a new show went from writing 2,000 lines to writing a config and a few

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.080
prompts. That part still feels like cheating. The cost curves made it possible. Newer model cut

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:24.640
per episode expense in half. Voices dropped to roughly $136 the old price, about $4 per million

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:31.000
characters. That single change is why two people can keep 14 shows running. I kept asking where the

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.520
reliability would break. We ended up with more than 3.5,000 automated tests before every publish,

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.300
plus an external dispatcher because GitHub Scheduler was too slow. And the human listen gate stayed

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:47.860
non-negotiable. No audio change ships without one of us hearing it first. The network went

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.320
multilingual early. Two Russian language shows launch with their own custom Russian voice.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:57.800
Every English episode now translates and revoices automatically into French, Russian, Spanish,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:03.280
and Chinese for about 18 cents in episode. Each language carries its own subscribeable feed,

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.900
plus a Russian dubbed YouTube channel. Every episode also publishes a blog post containing the

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:14.400
full transcript and chapters. Early posts carry the identical title, the show name, which killed

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:21.680
search visibility. Each post is now titled by the episodes hook. Full videos and shorts are auto-produced

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:28.480
with AI generated imagery. Per word captions timed from speech recognition, a heuristic that starts

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:35.000
each short at the most engaging beat, and thumbnails that auto shrink text to fit. YouTube's quota of

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.300
10,000 API units per day once forced brutal choices about which shows recede video. An increase to

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:47.940
200,000 units now allows all 14 shows to publish, roughly two dozen uploads daily. Retention data

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:54.460
feeds back into how titles are written. Reliability required its own war stories. A management dashboard

00:03:54.460 --> 00:04:00.800
tracks every known landmine in the system. The running list of past mistakes and how each was fixed.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:06.660
Duplicate guards prevent a late backup trigger from double publishing and automatic recovery,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:13.060
pull requests handle cases when a push fails mid-publish. Okay, but if we're scaling, what actually stays

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:18.740
load-bearing? The listen gates, the review ledger, the no fabrication rule, I still think the ledger

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:24.780
is the one we can't fully hand off. Fair. The seeded template problem taught us that. Give the model

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:30.440
one example phrase and it repeats it forever. We had to learn to decede by shape instead.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:37.500
That rule came from watching it fail in real time. A review agent audits one program twice a week,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.700
records predictions in a ledger, and the next review scores those predictions as hits or misses.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:49.580
The reviewer itself moved from an expensive model to a cheap one, roughly 30 cents a run instead of

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:55.900
$6 to $9. 10 shows existed at the first network review. Two narrative programs driven by topic

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:01.760
cues rather than news were added next. First principles daily built around the magic wand number and

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:08.960
the idiot index and unintended consequences. SpaceX daily launched the day of the IPO. The 14th show

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.760
is the one airing now. Open questions remain. What does it mean that two people can now do what once

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.900
required a media company? Where does human judgments stay load bearing? The listen gates,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:26.000
the editorial standards, the review ledger, and which of these could never be automated? What breaks

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:33.240
first at 50 shows? Is AI made media honest enough and does full disclosure plus no fabrication rules

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.780
actually earn trust? I keep coming back to the builder listening today. The cost curves show that

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:44.000
the expensive parts can collapse fast if you own the pipeline. The part that doesn't collapse is the

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:50.100
judgment on what stays honest. That brings us to the lever. Take the pledge at nerranetwork.com and

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:55.020
send this episode to one person who's been doom scrolling. It costs one minute and zero dollars.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.680
If even 1% of early listeners do the same, the starting point becomes a small club inside a week.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:07.280
The awkwardness is the real barrier. Keep the message short. This is the first episode of something new,

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:13.120
worth 10 minutes. Now the due positive dispatch. I'm doing this lever myself this week. I'll report

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.980
back on what happened. Send us what you tried. We'll read the first ones on air. We made an anthem for

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:23.540
the club. It's called do positive. We're playing the whole thing to close the very first episode.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:29.040
Stay for it. That wraps today's DP pod. Subscribe wherever you listen so tomorrow's good news

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.460
finds you. And when you pull today's lever right in and tell us, the dispatch only works if you do.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:41.860
I'm Patrick Novak. I'm Dan Perra. Do something about it. This episode used AI voice synthesis of our

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:45.980
voices. The editorial selection and analysis are our own.
